; host/bootstrap.bl — the pinned HOST MODULE for `Elixir.BeamLisp.Bootstrap`.
;
; This file is not a namespace. It is a `.bl` SOURCE that the bootstrap seed
; compiles with `BeamLisp.AOT/compile_host_file` into ONE exact-named beam
; (`Elixir.BeamLisp.Bootstrap.beam`) whose top-level `defn`s are plain exported
; functions — the exact call surface every caller already names at
; `Elixir.BeamLisp.Bootstrap`, so no caller is renamed. It is the `.bl` port of
; the recalled `lib/beam_lisp/bootstrap.ex`, and its public surface is that
; module's recalled one: `install!/1`, `install!/2`, `install_hosts!/1`,
; `install_hosts!/2`, `seed_dir/0`, `manifest/0`, `key_matches?/1`.
;
; (This wave owns source and tests only; the seed entry that points the real
; atom at this file, and the deletion of `bootstrap.ex`, are written by the
; wave that integrates it. The committed seed therefore still maps this atom to
; the Elixir beam for now — this port is judged by its focused test, which
; compiles it at a probe atom and against the live module.)
;
; WHY A PINNED MODULE AND NOT A NAMESPACE. `Bootstrap` is called from the
; `bin/bl` boot program BEFORE `Application.ensure_all_started(:beam_lisp)`:
; `install_hosts!/1` puts the pinned host leaves on the code path, `manifest/0`
; reports the floor's identity, and `install!/1` stages the compiler floor in a
; child VM's rebuild. All of that runs on a PREVIOUS generation's beams, before
; `BeamLisp.init/0` has interned a single var, so this module can never itself
; be an AOT-rebuilt `BeamLisp.Ns.*` namespace (the bootstrap circle). It is a
; pinned host beam: a fixed artifact named by the seed manifest, built by one
; generation and used by the next.
;
; ── THE PREBOOT CONSTRAINT: NO Rt, NO core, NO Ns.* ─────────────────────────
;
; `install_hosts!/1` runs with nothing on the code path but Elixir/OTP, this
; beam, and whatever the seed carries. `BeamLisp.Ns.Rt` is not loaded and
; `core`'s vars are not interned — they are interned BY the boot this module
; enables. So every BL convenience that compiles to a call into the runtime is
; forbidden, and the rule is sharper than "does not call the shim": a bare
; symbol in any expression position compiles to a var lookup through
; `BeamLisp.Env`, and a `(list …)`/`(str …)`/`(get …)` call compiles to a
; `BeamLisp.Ns.Core`/`BeamLisp.Ns.Rt` call. Measured, a pinned host that names
; an unbound helper `a` emitted `BeamLisp.Env/fetch!`.
;
; The surface that IS safe, and that this file is written in:
;
;   * `'(…)` — a quoted list compiles to a LIST LITERAL, free. Used for the
;     fixed argument lists of BIF/host calls (`'(:attributes)`, `'(:positive)`,
;     `'(:md5)`) and the empty list `'()`. A `(list …)` call would not be.
;
;   * `erlang/==`, `erlang/is_binary`, `erlang/is_list`, `erlang/is_tuple`,
;     `erlang/is_map`, `erlang/is_map_key`, `erlang/element`, `erlang/hd`,
;     `erlang/length`, `erlang/map_get`, `erlang/list_to_binary`,
;     `erlang/list_to_tuple`, `erlang/binary_to_list`, `erlang/byte_size`,
;     `erlang/unique_integer`, `erlang/module_loaded`, `erlang/apply`,
;     `erlang/error`, `erlang/++` are BIFs. `=`, `some?`, `nil?`, `str`,
;     `first`, `count`, `get`, `assoc`, `cons`, `vec`, `concat`, `keyword?`,
;     `map?` … would each compile to a `BeamLisp.Ns.Rt` call or a `core` var
;     fetch, so none appears. (The macros `cond`/`when`/`or`/`and`/`not`/
;     `if`/`let`/`fn`/`loop` are FINE — they expand at compile time into
;     `if`/`let`, which emit nothing at runtime.)
;
;   * `Enum`, `Path`, `File`, `Code`, `Keyword`, `Map`, `MapSet`, `String`,
;     `Macro`, `Integer`, `Kernel`, `Application`, `Base`, `crypto` and
;     `:code`/`:beam_lib` are host modules: Elixir/OTP, on the path in any
;     `elixir` VM, and the same modules the Elixir twin called. `Enum` invokes
;     the BL closures below as plain funs, exactly as it invoked the Elixir
;     captures it replaces.
;
;   * LIST AND TUPLE BUILDING is `cat`/`tup` below. Both are VARIADIC: a call
;     `(cat a b c)` lowers to a direct sibling call whose rest-argument list is
;     built as Core cons cells at the call site, NOT a `(list …)` call — so the
;     emitted code names no runtime function (probed: only `cat__bl_v` and
;     `:erlang.iolist_to_binary` appear). `cat` joins an iolist to one binary
;     without `str`; `tup` builds a real Erlang tuple from the same rest list
;     without `(tuple …)`. This is why the messages below stay byte-identical
;     to the recalled ones without ever touching the language's var registry.
;
; ── ABI PARITY IS THE CONTRACT ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Every function below reproduces its Elixir counterpart clause for clause: the
; same file operations in the same order, the same return types
; (`:ok`/`{:staged, :compiler_key_mismatch}`), the same exception class and
; message (a corrupt/missing seed is a `RuntimeError` with the recalled text).
;
; The PUBLIC surface is exactly the recalled module's minus the Elixir-generated
; `__info__/1` (which a pinned beam does not carry). One deliberate deviation is
; recorded here rather than hidden: `defn-` is NOT privatized on the pinned-host
; path — the emitter exports EVERY top-level defn it compiles — so the private
; helpers below (`cat`, `tup`, `stamped_key`, `loaded_is?`,
; `seed_module_to_ns`, …, `install_one`) appear as extra exports. They carry no
; caller-visible behaviour and no caller names them; `test/bl/bootstrap_test.bl`
; pins the public surface AND the callable helpers. (The `tiers`/`reload_watcher`
; ports kept their surface exact by making every private helper a local closure;
; that was tractable for one or two shared helpers — this module shares sixteen
; across both install paths, and rebuilding them as duplicate closures in each
; caller would be a larger, less reviewable change than the extra exports it
; avoids.)
;
; Run: bl test test/bl/bootstrap_test.bl

(ns host.bootstrap)

; ── preboot-safe list/binary/tuple building ─────────────────────────────────
;
; `cat`/`tup` exist for one reason: their variadic rest list is built as Core
; cons cells, so they can join binaries and build tuples WITHOUT `str`/`list`/
; `tuple` — each of which resolves through the language's var registry and is
; absent at preboot.

(defn- cat
  "Join an iolist of binaries into one binary, without `str` (which resolves
   through the language's var registry, `BeamLisp.Env`). Every piece is already
   a binary/iolist, so `iolist_to_binary` is the exact join with no Env in the
   path."
  [& parts]
  (erlang/iolist_to_binary parts))

(defn- tup
  "Build a real Erlang tuple from the variadic arguments, without `(tuple …)`
   (a runtime call). Used for the `{:staged, :compiler_key_mismatch}` result
   and the `{order, name}` sort key."
  [& xs]
  (erlang/list_to_tuple xs))

; ── the manifest ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn- read_manifest!
  "The committed seed's manifest as data. `Code.eval_file/1` returns
   `{value, bindings}`; a manifest is an Elixir map literal, so `element/1`."
  [path]
  (erlang/element 1 (Code/eval_file path)))

(defn- sha256
  "Lowercase hex sha256, the digest the manifest records."
  [bytes]
  (Base/encode16 (crypto/hash :sha256 bytes) (List/wrap (tup :case :lower))))

(defn- verify_seed_file!
  "Raise unless `src` exists and hashes to `want_sha`. A missing or corrupt seed
   is fatal — there is no genesis compiler behind it."
  [src name want_sha]
  (if (File/exists? src)
    (let [got (sha256 (File/read! src))]
      (if (erlang/== got want_sha)
        :ok
        (erlang/error
          (Elixir.RuntimeError/exception
            (cat "beam-lisp bootstrap seed is corrupt: " name " checksum mismatch.\n"
                 "  manifest: " want_sha "\n"
                 "  on disk:  " got "\n")))))
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.RuntimeError/exception
        (cat "beam-lisp bootstrap seed is incomplete: " name
             " missing from " (seed_dir))))))

; ── install mechanics ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn- loaded_is?
  "Whether `mod`'s CURRENT code is exactly `bytes` — compared by the beam's md5,
   which is what the loader itself records. `mod.module_info(:md5)` is reached by
   `erlang/apply/3`, never a dynamic variable reference."
  [mod bytes]
  (and (erlang/module_loaded mod)
       (let [r (beam_lib/md5 bytes)]
         (if (and (erlang/is_tuple r) (erlang/== :ok (erlang/element 1 r)))
           (erlang/== (erlang/apply mod :module_info '(:md5))
                      (erlang/element 2 (erlang/element 2 r)))
           false))))

(defn- stamped_key
  "A beam's `bl_compiler_key` attribute, read WITHOUT loading it — the attribute
   `AOT.emit_module` stamps into every namespace beam beside
   `__bl_provenance__/0`. Loading the beam to ask about a file would replace the
   live module under a running VM."
  [path]
  (let [r (beam_lib/chunks (erlang/binary_to_list path) '(:attributes))]
    (if (and (erlang/is_tuple r) (erlang/== :ok (erlang/element 1 r)))
      (let [chunks (erlang/element 2 (erlang/element 2 r))
            attrs (erlang/element 2 (erlang/hd chunks))
            v (Keyword/get attrs :bl_compiler_key)]
        (cond
          (and (erlang/is_list v)
               (erlang/== 1 (erlang/length v))
               (erlang/is_binary (erlang/hd v))) (erlang/hd v)
          (erlang/is_binary v) v
          :else nil))
      nil)))

(defn- install_one
  "Copy the seed beam into ebin only when the destination is absent or drifted,
   so a warm build does no work. Write-then-rename keeps a reader from ever
   seeing a half-written beam."
  [src dst want_sha]
  (let [r (File/read dst)
        current (if (and (erlang/is_tuple r) (erlang/== :ok (erlang/element 1 r)))
                  (sha256 (erlang/element 2 r))
                  nil)]
    (if (erlang/== current want_sha)
      :ok
      (let [tmp (cat dst ".tmp-"
                     (Integer/to_string (erlang/unique_integer '(:positive))))]
        (File/cp! src tmp)
        (File/rename! tmp dst)))))

; ── seed beam names -> namespace / tier key ─────────────────────────────────

(defn- seed_module_to_ns
  "Map a committed seed beam filename to the beam-lisp namespace it provides, or
   nil for a companion module (Body.*/Init.*) that is not a namespace of its own.
   `Elixir.BeamLisp.Ns.Compiler.beam` -> \"compiler\";
   `Elixir.BeamLisp.Ns.Reader-node.beam` -> \"reader-node\". Only the top-level
   `BeamLisp.Ns.<Name>` shims name a namespace; the drift gate keys on those."
  [filename]
  (let [base (Path/basename filename ".beam")]
    (if (String/starts_with? base "Elixir.BeamLisp.Ns.")
      (let [rest (String/replace_prefix base "Elixir.BeamLisp.Ns." "")]
        (if (String/contains? rest ".")
          nil
          (Enum/map_join (String/split rest "-") "-"
                         (fn [s] (Macro/underscore s)))))
      nil)))

(defn- seed_module_ns
  "The namespace ANY seed beam shape belongs to: `Ns.X`, `Ns.Body.X` and
   `Ns.Init.X` are one generation unit, and the tier key is a property of the
   namespace, so the companions answer like their shim."
  [filename]
  (let [base (Path/basename filename ".beam")
        core1 (String/replace_prefix base "Elixir.BeamLisp.Ns." "")
        core2 (String/replace_prefix core1 "Body." "")
        core3 (String/replace_prefix core2 "Init." "")]
    (Enum/map_join (String/split core3 "-") "-"
                   (fn [s] (Macro/underscore s)))))

(defn- expected_key
  "The tier key a seed beam is expected to carry. The seed spans both toolchain
   tiers — the codegen (`priv/boot/`) and the build driver (`priv/build/`) — so
   one key cannot judge it: a driver beam is stamped with `build_key/0` and a
   codegen beam with `compiler_key/0`. Kept in step with the emitter through
   `AOTCache.key_for_ns/1`."
  [name]
  (BeamLisp.AOTCache/key_for_ns (seed_module_ns name)))

; ── the host inventory ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn- host_leaf?
  "A pinned HOST leaf as the LEGACY name rule spells it: an `Elixir.BeamLisp.*`
   module that is NOT part of the namespace generation ladder
   (`Elixir.BeamLisp.Ns.*`). The FALLBACK for a floor built before the manifest
   carried an explicit `hosts` list."
  [name]
  (and (String/starts_with? name "Elixir.BeamLisp.")
       (erlang/not (String/starts_with? name "Elixir.BeamLisp.Ns."))))

(defn- legacy_host_names
  "The pinned host beams of a floor too old to carry an explicit `hosts` list:
   the `Elixir.BeamLisp.*` non-`Ns.*` names."
  [manifest]
  (Enum/filter (Map/keys (Map/get manifest "modules" (maps/new)))
               (fn [n] (host_leaf? n))))

(defn- validate_hosts!
  "Refuse a malformed pinned-host inventory BEFORE any byte is read: every name
   must be a binary, name a beam this floor carries (a key in `modules`), be a
   plain filename ending `.beam`, not be part of the `Elixir.BeamLisp.Ns.*`
   generation ladder, and appear at most once. A malformed inventory is a floor
   nobody can trust: it is REFUSED, never guessed around."
  [hosts manifest]
  (let [modules (Map/get manifest "modules" (maps/new))]
    (Enum/each hosts
      (fn [name]
        (cond
          (erlang/not (erlang/is_binary name))
          (erlang/error
            (Elixir.RuntimeError/exception
              (cat "beam-lisp bootstrap seed manifest lists a malformed pinned host: "
                   (Kernel/inspect name))))

          (String/starts_with? name "Elixir.BeamLisp.Ns.")
          (erlang/error
            (Elixir.RuntimeError/exception
              (cat "beam-lisp bootstrap seed manifest lists a generation-ladder module "
                   "as a pinned host: " name)))

          (or (erlang/not (erlang/== (Path/basename name) name))
              (erlang/not (String/ends_with? name ".beam")))
          (erlang/error
            (Elixir.RuntimeError/exception
              (cat "beam-lisp bootstrap seed manifest lists a malformed pinned host name: "
                   (Kernel/inspect name))))

          (erlang/not (Map/has_key? modules name))
          (erlang/error
            (Elixir.RuntimeError/exception
              (cat "beam-lisp bootstrap seed manifest lists pinned host " name
                   " absent from its \"modules\" map")))

          :else
          :ok)))
    (if (erlang/== (Enum/uniq hosts) hosts)
      hosts
      (erlang/error
        (Elixir.RuntimeError/exception
          "beam-lisp bootstrap seed manifest lists a pinned host more than once")))))

(defn- host_names
  "The pinned host beams a floor carries, AS NAMED BY ITS MANIFEST.

   A manifest with an explicit `hosts` list is the AUTHORITY: host selection is
   membership in that list, not a name prefix, so a pinned leaf OUTSIDE
   `Elixir.BeamLisp.*` — `Elixir.Enumerable.BeamLisp.Set`, the protocol-impl
   module compiled beside its host source — can be staged. An OLDER floor
   (built before this field existed) carries no `hosts` key: fall back to the
   prefix rule those floors were built under."
  [manifest]
  (let [hosts (Map/get manifest "hosts")]
    (cond
      (erlang/is_list hosts) (validate_hosts! hosts manifest)
      (Map/has_key? manifest "hosts")
      (erlang/error
        (Elixir.RuntimeError/exception
          (cat "beam-lisp bootstrap seed manifest's \"hosts\" is not a list: "
               (Kernel/inspect hosts))))
      :else (legacy_host_names manifest))))

; ── the generation rule (for the `Ns.*` generation unit only) ────────────────

(defn- fresh_generation?
  "Whether `dst` holds a beam stamped with the current toolchain key. The stamp
   is the `__bl_provenance__/0` function, which only the namespace SHIM
   (Ns.<Name>) carries, so a Body/Init destination inherits its sibling shim's
   verdict. The sibling verdict only inherits when the destination beam is there
   AND was written in the same pass as the shim (shim, body and init are one
   generation unit): a killed build can leave a fresh shim beside a stale body,
   and trusting the shim alone then skips the reinstall. The stamp is read from
   the beam's ATTRIBUTES chunk, never by loading it."
  [dst current_key]
  (let [provenance_beam
        (cond
          (String/contains? dst ".Body.") (String/replace dst ".Body." ".")
          (String/contains? dst ".Init.") (String/replace dst ".Init." ".")
          :else dst)
        unit_consistent?
        (or (erlang/== provenance_beam dst)
            (and (File/exists? dst)
                 (File/exists? provenance_beam)
                 (erlang/>= (erlang/map_get :mtime (File/stat! dst))
                            (erlang/map_get :mtime (File/stat! provenance_beam)))))]
    (if unit_consistent?
      (erlang/== (stamped_key provenance_beam) current_key)
      false)))

(defn- maybe_install_one
  "The generation rule for the `Ns.*` GENERATION UNIT: the seed is a FLOOR,
   never a ceiling — a destination beam stamped with the CURRENT toolchain key
   is a fresh gen-N+1 rebuild and strictly supersedes the gen-N seed, so it is
   kept. Pinned HOST leaves never reach here (they are sha-authoritative)."
  [src dst want_sha current_key]
  (if (fresh_generation? dst current_key)
    :ok
    (install_one src dst want_sha)))

; ── the tier authority ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn- ensure_tier_authority!
  "Put the pinned tier authority (`Elixir.BeamLisp.Tiers`) on the code path
   BEFORE any key is computed. `AOTCache.compiler_key/0` asks it where the
   codegen sources live and which tier a namespace is in, so no key may be
   computed before it is loaded — and once `tiers.ex` is gone this SEED beam is
   the only copy of the module a boot has.

   The destination is preferred when it already holds a beam (a fresh gen-N+1
   rebuild supersedes the seed floor); otherwise the seed, sha-verified before
   it is trusted to answer a key question. A floor frozen before `Tiers` was
   pinned carries no such entry: leave whatever the code path already has."
  [seed_dir ebin manifest]
  (let [name "Elixir.BeamLisp.Tiers.beam"
        modules (Map/get manifest "modules")
        want_sha (if (erlang/is_map modules) (Map/get modules name) nil)]
    (if (erlang/is_binary want_sha)
      (let [src (Path/join seed_dir name)
            dst (Path/join ebin name)]
        (verify_seed_file! src name want_sha)
        (let [path (if (File/exists? dst) dst src)
              mod (String/to_atom (Path/basename name ".beam"))
              bytes (File/read! path)]
          (if (loaded_is? mod bytes)
            :ok
            (do
              (code/purge mod)
              (let [r (code/load_binary mod (erlang/binary_to_list path) bytes)]
                (if (and (erlang/is_tuple r)
                         (erlang/== :module (erlang/element 1 r))
                         (erlang/== mod (erlang/element 2 r)))
                  :ok
                  (erlang/error
                    (Elixir.RuntimeError/exception
                      (cat "cannot load pinned tier authority " name ": "
                           (Kernel/inspect r))))))))))
      :ok)))

; ── the public surface ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn seed_dir
  "Absolute path to the committed seed directory (best-effort, dev + release)."
  []
  (let [dir (code/priv_dir :beam_lisp)]
    (if (erlang/is_list dir)
      (Path/join (erlang/list_to_binary dir) "bootstrap/seed")
      (Path/join '("priv" "bootstrap/seed")))))

(defn manifest
  "The committed seed's manifest as data, or nil when there is no seed.

   The SEED'S IDENTITY is reported, never inferred: `bl doctor --deep` answers
   \"which toolchain built this floor?\" by reading these keys."
  []
  (let [path (Path/join (seed_dir) "manifest.exs")]
    (if (File/exists? path) (read_manifest! path) nil)))

(defn key_matches?
  "Whether the committed seed's keys match this toolchain's. Public because the
   answer is a FACT ABOUT THE TREE, not private bookkeeping: `bl doctor --deep`
   reports it."
  [manifest]
  (and (erlang/== (Map/get manifest "compiler_key") (BeamLisp.AOTCache/compiler_key))
       (erlang/== (Map/get manifest "build_key") (BeamLisp.AOTCache/build_key))))

(defn install!
  "Ensure the compiler seed is present and valid in `compile_path`'s ebin.

   Returns `:ok` when the seed is installed (or already current), or
   `{:staged, :compiler_key_mismatch}` when a stale-key floor was installed as
   a previous-generation bootstrap stage for the rebuild. Raises when the
   committed seed is missing or corrupt — there is no genesis fallback."
  ([compile_path] (install! compile_path '()))
  ([compile_path opts]
   (let [seed_dir (Keyword/get opts :seed_dir (seed_dir))
         manifest_path (Path/join seed_dir "manifest.exs")]
     (if (File/exists? manifest_path)
       (let [manifest (read_manifest! manifest_path)
             ebin (String.Chars/to_string compile_path)]
         (File/mkdir_p! ebin)

         ;; THE APP RESOURCE FIRST — before the key is judged below.
         ;; `AOTCache.compiler_key/0` folds in the application's VSN, read
         ;; through `:application.load(:beam_lisp)`. On a checkout that has
         ;; never been built there is no `.app` yet, so the key was computed
         ;; with `vsn: "unknown"`, NEVER matched the floor, and every fresh
         ;; clone staged the seed (BUG-048). Installing the floor's app resource
         ;; first makes the key this tree's real key. A floor is a bootstrap,
         ;; not a correction: installed only when the destination has NONE.
         (let [app (Map/get manifest "app")]
           (if (and (erlang/is_map app)
                    (Map/has_key? app "file")
                    (Map/has_key? app "sha256"))
             (let [file (Map/get app "file")
                   want (Map/get app "sha256")
                   src (Path/join seed_dir file)
                   dst (Path/join ebin file)]
               (verify_seed_file! src file want)
               (if (File/exists? dst) :ok (File/cp! src dst)))
             :ok))

         ;; The tier authority is loaded BEFORE the key is judged:
         ;; `key_matches?/1` calls `compiler_key/0`, which needs `Tiers`.
         (ensure_tier_authority! seed_dir ebin manifest)

         (let [matches? (key_matches? manifest)
               hosts (MapSet/new (host_names manifest))]

           ;; ALWAYS install the committed seed beams — on a match AND on a
           ;; mismatch. A MATCH means byte-identical finished compiler. A
           ;; MISMATCH does NOT mean unusable: it is a WORKING compiler of the
           ;; PREVIOUS generation, and a self-hosted compiler is bootstrapped by
           ;; the previous generation of itself. `stale?/2` (aot.ex) then sees
           ;; the freshly built beams carry the current key and serves them.
           ;; Integrity (sha256) is verified regardless.
           (Enum/each (Map/get manifest "modules")
             (fn [pair]
               (let [name (erlang/element 1 pair)
                     want_sha (erlang/element 2 pair)
                     src (Path/join seed_dir name)
                     dst (Path/join ebin name)]
                 (verify_seed_file! src name want_sha)
                 ;; Pinned HOST leaves are sha-authoritative,
                 ;; NOT generation-gated: a host beam whose bytes changed
                 ;; without rotating the tier key would otherwise be kept stale
                 ;; forever, because `fresh_generation?/2` reads only the stamp
                 ;; and the stamp is unchanged.
                 (if (MapSet/member? hosts name)
                   (install_one src dst want_sha)
                   (maybe_install_one src dst want_sha (expected_key name))))))

           ;; Make the code server SEE the just-installed beams immediately: a
           ;; module already resolved to `:non_existing` stays cached as missing
           ;; until explicitly (re)loaded. A module whose loaded code IS these
           ;; bytes is left alone — reloading it would make the running version
           ;; "old", and the next purge would kill every closure it created (the
           ;; runtime's seeded `core` values among them).
           (Enum/each (Map/get manifest "modules")
             (fn [pair]
               (let [name (erlang/element 1 pair)
                     mod (String/to_atom (Path/basename name ".beam"))
                     beam_path (Path/join ebin name)
                     bytes (File/read! beam_path)]
                 (if (loaded_is? mod bytes)
                   :ok
                   (do
                     (code/purge mod)
                     (code/load_binary mod (erlang/binary_to_list beam_path) bytes))))))

           (if matches?
             (do
               ;; A matching seed is the finished compiler; no staging trust.
               (Application/delete_env :beam_lisp :bootstrap_staging)
               :ok)
             ;; A mismatched seed was installed as a PREVIOUS-generation
             ;; bootstrap stage. Record the namespaces it provides so the AOT
             ;; drift gate trusts THOSE staged beams for interning (interning
             ;; replays def VALUES; it does not recompile). The BUILD DRIVER is
             ;; never staged, even if a seed carries it: a staged driver is a
             ;; mixed toolchain, not a bootstrap.
             (let [staged_ns
                   (Enum/uniq
                     (Enum/reject
                       (Enum/reject
                         (Enum/map (Map/keys (Map/get manifest "modules"))
                                   (fn [n] (seed_module_to_ns n)))
                         (fn [x] (erlang/== nil x)))
                       (fn [x] (Enum/member? (BeamLisp.Tiers/build_namespaces) x))))]
               (Application/put_env :beam_lisp :bootstrap_staging staged_ns)
               (tup :staged :compiler_key_mismatch)))))
       (erlang/error
         (Elixir.RuntimeError/exception
           (cat "beam-lisp bootstrap seed is missing: " manifest_path " not found.\n"
                "\n"
                "The self-hosted compiler boots from a committed seed (the AOT-compiled\n"
                "`compiler`/`reader-node` closure). Without it there is no compiler to\n"
                "build the sources. If you are developing beam-lisp itself, rebuild and\n"
                "commit the seed (`bl seed`, after a keyed build); otherwise your checkout\n"
                "is incomplete.\n")))))))

(defn install_hosts!
  "Install the pinned host portion of the seed without starting the language.

   A normal `bl build` recompiles the remaining Elixir substrate and may leave
   an ebin with no pinned host beam. The boot-tier shims can be current, so the
   full `install!/1` (run only in the rebuild child) is not called on this path.
   Install the exact-name host leaves before application startup instead: the
   compiler calls them while the compiler namespace is being interned.

   Host install is sha-authoritative — no key is computed on this path."
  ([compile_path] (install_hosts! compile_path '()))
  ([compile_path opts]
   (let [seed_dir (Keyword/get opts :seed_dir (seed_dir))
         manifest_path (Path/join seed_dir "manifest.exs")
         manifest (read_manifest! manifest_path)
         ebin (String.Chars/to_string compile_path)]
     (File/mkdir_p! ebin)

     ;; The tier authority is loaded first so the pinned leaf is on the code
     ;; path before anything asks a tier question of it.
     (ensure_tier_authority! seed_dir ebin manifest)

     ;; The host inventory is the MANIFEST's list (`host_names/1`), not a name
     ;; prefix. Deterministic install order: the authority first, then by name —
     ;; the authority must be on the code path before anything asks it a tier
     ;; question.
     (let [modules (Map/get manifest "modules")]
       (Enum/each
         (Enum/sort_by (host_names manifest)
           (fn [name]
             (tup (if (erlang/== name "Elixir.BeamLisp.Tiers.beam") 0 1) name)))
         (fn [name]
           (let [want_sha (Map/fetch! modules name)
                 src (Path/join seed_dir name)
                 dst (Path/join ebin name)]
             (verify_seed_file! src name want_sha)
             ;; Host leaves are sha-authoritative: the seed is the pinned
             ;; artifact, so its bytes win whenever the destination differs —
             ;; even when the destination carries a MATCHING `bl_compiler_key`.
             (install_one src dst want_sha)
             (let [mod (String/to_atom (Path/basename name ".beam"))
                   bytes (File/read! dst)]
               (if (loaded_is? mod bytes)
                 :ok
                 (do
                   (code/purge mod)
                   (let [r (code/load_binary mod (erlang/binary_to_list dst) bytes)]
                     (if (and (erlang/is_tuple r)
                              (erlang/== :module (erlang/element 1 r))
                              (erlang/== mod (erlang/element 2 r)))
                       :ok
                       (erlang/error
                         (Elixir.RuntimeError/exception
                           (cat "cannot load pinned host " name ": "
                                (Kernel/inspect r))))))))))))
       :ok))))
